  "query_preview": "..\n\nExpansion of high pressure across the upper Midwest will progress a \ncold front into southern Michigan where the boundary is then \nexpected to slow and stall out tonight. This will retain an area of \nenhanced theta-e across southern Michigan overnight, characterized \nby PW values nearing 2\" closer to the MI/OH border along with 850mb \ndew points ranging between 12-14C. Low level convergence and weak \nshortwave impulses within this region of higher moisture will be the \nfocal point for renewed showers and possibly thunderstorms this \nevening through at least tomorrow morning. \n\nFor tonight, expansion of renewed showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwill be more favorable around and south of M59. Any arrival of \nconvection between 23Z - 02Z may be able to capitalize on a brief \nincrease in surface instability along the nose of enhanced theta-e \nahead of the cold front. This coupled with moderate low to mid level \nlapse rates and 0-6km bulk shear around 35 knots will bring some low \nend chances for an isolated strong to severe thunderstorm. Any \nstronger development will be capable of producing localized stronger \nwind gusts from a wet microburst. SR-hodographs also show a modest \nstreamwise component from 0-3 km, highlighting potential for some \nbetter organization, but all said, poor buoyancy with unfavorable \ntiming of convective potential will be a major hindrance to any \nrobust convection. SWODY1 highlights a marginal risk for several \nweather for this activity.  \n\nSevere chances are then mitigated overnight once surface instability \nwanes and conditions turn more elevated. Zonal low to mid-level flow \nwill bring continuation of shower and storm potential overnight \nthrough tomorrow afternoon. Any repeated shower and thunderstorm \nwill have the low potential to bring localized flooding in flood \nprone locations. See the hydrology section for additional details. \n\nThe frontal boundary will waver across southern Michigan late \nWednesday into Thursday, providing additional chances for showers \nand thunderstorms through the midweek period. Some respite from rain \nchances will be likely late Thursday into Friday as the southern \nperiphery of high pressure builds in across the Great Lakes, but \nadditional chances for showers and storms, possibly heavy rain, will \nre-enter on Saturday as storm complex derives from the Plains \nprogresses into a stronger baroclinic zone. There is uncertainty \nthis far out regarding where any enhanced forcing will align, with \nmid-range models highlighting anywhere from central Michigan to \nlocations south into the Ohio Valley as potential zones for elevated \nQPF.",
